A benchmark tool, and the 1,360x outlier it found on the first run

tools/bench_engines.py measures the whole engine contract - filter,
filter_nested, project, group_by, sort, limit, join - on every engine that
constructs, with a median of several repeats and the spread printed
beside it.

The spread is the point. A benchmark reporting one number measures the
scheduler: DuckDB's group-by was 53.7 ms and 293.6 ms on two Colab runs of
identical code. So every figure here carries its spread, a ratio smaller
than the spread is called noise rather than a result, and each result is
fingerprinted so a fast *wrong* answer cannot be reported as a win.

The data is deliberately wide. Narrow data hides a real cost - an engine
that copies every column regardless of projection looks identical to one
that pushes the projection down, as long as the group-by needs every column
anyway - so eight `pad` columns are added that nothing reads.

IT FOUND A 3,936x OUTLIER ON THE FIRST RUN.

  operation      engine         median ms   x fastest
  filter_nested  arrow     20,113.87 -> 14.79   3936x -> 1.0x
  group_by       pandas          1,493.07     161x
  group_by       arrow            1,526.29     159x
  join           pandas          4,459.10     159x
  join           arrow            4,622.24     159x

ArrowEngine._vector_filter handled exactly one shape: a single comparison
with a column on the left. A conjunction fell through to _row_filter,
which materialises every row as a Python dict and calls a Python predicate
per row - 20 seconds for 500,000 rows, reproducible to within 0.4%. The
single-comparison case measured 17 ms and looked fine, which is exactly why
it survived; the benchmark had to try the *nested* shape to see it.

It is now recursive: AND/OR, null tests and nested arithmetic all take the
Arrow kernel path, using and_kleene/or_kleene so a null operand stays
unknown rather than silently becoming false. Returning None still means
"use the row path", so an exotic predicate is slower rather than wrong.

This is the third time the same shape of defect has appeared - pandas
frame.apply, then cuDF query, then this - which is the argument for having
a benchmark rather than a list of known slow things.

MEASURED AND STILL OPEN, not fixed here:
- arrow/pandas join ~4.5 s, 159x. ArrowEngine.join is a hand-written
  Python hash join; pyarrow has had a native Table.join for years.
- arrow/pandas group_by ~1.5 s, 160x. Both delegate to the same Python
  group-by fallback.

594 passed, 7 skipped.
